const a={moduleId:"case_uses",title:"Noun Case Uses",summary:"Each case does several jobs. To name the job, look at the noun's meaning (a person, a thing, a time, a place), any preposition, and the verb or adjective it goes with.",sections:[{heading:"Ablative: Means, Manner, Accompaniment",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'Three ablatives all translate as "with", so ask what kind of "with" it is. **Means** is the tool or thing used, with **no preposition**. **Manner** says **how** something is done; it takes **cum**, but when the noun has an adjective, cum may be left out or placed between the adjective and the noun. **Accompaniment** is a person who goes along, with **cum**.'},{kind:"table",head:["Use","Signal","Example","English"],rows:[["Means","a thing used; no preposition","Puer canem **baculō** pepulit.","The boy drove off the dog with a stick."],["Manner","how it was done; cum, or adjective + (cum)","Epistulam **magnā cum cūrā** scrīpsit.","He wrote the letter with great care."],["Accompaniment","cum + a person","Mārcus **cum frātre** ambulābat.","Marcus was walking with his brother."]]},{kind:"p",text:'Means **never** takes cum. Without an adjective, manner needs cum: **cum cūrā**, "carefully". With an adjective, both **magnā cum cūrā** and **magnā cūrā** are good Latin.'}]},{heading:"Ablative: Time When and Time Within Which",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:"A plain ablative of a time word, with no preposition, says **when** something happened, or the period **within which** it happened."},{kind:"table",head:["Use","Signal","Example","English"],rows:[["Time when","a point in time","**Mediā nocte** profectī sunt.","They set out at midnight."],["Time within which","a stretch of time","**Duābus hōrīs** redībō.","I will return within two hours."]]},{kind:"p",text:"How **long** something lasts is not ablative but accusative (see the last section)."}]},{heading:"Ablative: Agent, Comparison, Separation, Cause; Ablative of Description",blocks:[{kind:"table",head:["Use","Signal","Example","English"],rows:[["Agent","ā/ab + a person, with a passive verb","Urbs **ā Gallīs** capta est.","The city was captured by the Gauls."],["Comparison","after a comparative, instead of quam","Ferrum **aurō** dūrius est.","Iron is harder than gold."],["Separation","verbs of freeing, depriving, keeping away","Cōnsul cīvēs **metū** līberāvit.","The consul freed the citizens from fear."],["Cause","the reason, often with verbs of feeling","Puella **dolōre** lacrimābat.","The girl was weeping from grief."],["Description","noun + adjective describing a person","Senex erat **longā barbā**.","The old man had a long beard."]]},{kind:"p",text:"The agent is always a **person** (or animal) and needs **ā/ab**. A thing that causes a passive action is a bare ablative of means. When the first thing compared is nominative or accusative, the ablative of comparison can replace quam + the same case: *Ferrum dūrius est quam aurum* says the same thing. With separation, a preposition (ā, ab, ē, ex) is sometimes added, and it is regular when the separation is from a person."}]},{heading:"Dative: Indirect Object, Special Verbs, Possession, Purpose and Reference",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:"The dative names the person (or thing) **to** or **for** whom something is, or is done."},{kind:"table",head:["Use","Signal","Example","English"],rows:[["Indirect object","verbs of giving, showing, telling","Pater **fīliō** equum dedit.","The father gave a horse to his son."],["With a special verb","pāreō, crēdō, parcō, noceō, faveō, persuādeō...","Mīlitēs **ducī** pāruērunt.","The soldiers obeyed their general."],["Possession","dative + a form of sum","**Huic puerō** est canis.","This boy has a dog."],["Purpose","the job something does; often a double dative","Haec rēs **mihi cūrae** est.","This matter is a concern to me."],["Reference","the person who benefits or is concerned","Nōn **mihi** sed **patriae** labōrō.","I work not for myself but for my country."]]},{kind:"p",text:'Special verbs take a dative where English has a direct object, so learn them as a list. A **double dative** pairs a dative of purpose with a dative of the person concerned: in *mihi cūrae*, cūrae is purpose ("as a concern") and mihi says to whom.'}]},{heading:"Genitive: Partitive and Description",blocks:[{kind:"table",head:["Use","Signal","Example","English"],rows:[["Partitive","the whole that a part is taken from","Quis **vestrum** hoc vīdit?","Which of you saw this?"],["Partitive","after words of quantity: satis, nihil, pars","Satis **pecūniae** nōn habēmus.","We do not have enough money."],["Description","noun + adjective describing a person","Erat homō **magnae prūdentiae**.","He was a man of great good sense."],["Description","measure or age, with a number","Puer **decem annōrum** aderat.","A boy ten years old was present."]]},{kind:"p",text:'The partitive forms of the pronouns are **nostrum** and **vestrum** ("of us", "of you"). With numbers Latin usually uses **ex** or **dē** instead of a partitive genitive: *ūnus ex mīlitibus*, "one of the soldiers".'}]},{heading:"Accusative: Extent and Place to Which",blocks:[{kind:"table",head:["Use","Signal","Example","English"],rows:[["Extent of time","how long","**Multōs annōs** Rōmae habitāvit.","He lived in Rome for many years."],["Extent of space","how far, how high, how wide","Fossa **vīgintī pedēs** lāta erat.","The ditch was twenty feet wide."],["Place to which","in or ad + accusative","Nautae **in Graeciam** nāvigāvērunt.","The sailors sailed to Greece."],["Place to which","city, small island, domus, rūs: no preposition","Mercātor **Corinthum** nāvigāvit.","The merchant sailed to Corinth."]]},{kind:"tip",title:"Three common mix-ups",text:'**Time:** the accusative says **how long** (*tōtum diem*, "all day"); the ablative says **when** or **within which** (*eō diē*, "on that day"). **Agent or means:** a person doing a passive action is ā/ab + ablative, a thing used is a bare ablative: *Rēx ā servō venēnō necātus est*, "The king was killed by a slave with poison." **Description:** a quality with an adjective can be **genitive or ablative**, and the ablative needs no cum (*vir summā fortitūdine* or *vir summae fortitūdinis*, "a man of the greatest bravery"); a number or measure uses the genitive.'}]}],check:["m13-102","m13-202","m13-601","m13-602","m13-603"]},s={moduleId:"conditionals",title:"Conditionals",summary:'An "if" sentence is named by the mood and tense of its verbs. The simple and future more vivid types use the indicative; the future less vivid and the contrary-to-fact types use the subjunctive.',sections:[{heading:"Protasis, apodosis and the types",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'A conditional sentence has two halves. The **protasis** is the "if" clause, introduced by **sī** ("if") or **nisi** ("if not", "unless"). The **apodosis** is the main clause, the conclusion: "then...". To name the type, look at the mood and tense of the verb in each half.'},{kind:"table",caption:"Conditional types",head:["Type","Protasis (sī...)","Apodosis","English"],rows:[["Simple present","present indicative","present indicative","If he is..., he is..."],["Simple past","past indicative","past indicative","If he was..., he was..."],["Future more vivid","future or future perfect indicative","future indicative","If he does..., he will..."],["Future less vivid","present subjunctive","present subjunctive","If he should..., he would..."],["Present contrary to fact","imperfect subjunctive","imperfect subjunctive","If he were..., he would..."],["Past contrary to fact","pluperfect subjunctive","pluperfect subjunctive","If he had..., he would have..."],["Mixed contrary to fact","pluperfect subjunctive","imperfect subjunctive","If he had..., he would (now)..."]]},{kind:"p",text:'The negative of sī is usually **nisi**: "if ... not" or "unless". The ordinary negative **nōn** can also stand inside a sī clause.'}]},{heading:"Simple and future more vivid: the indicative",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'A **simple** condition makes no claim about whether the "if" is true; it just says that one thing goes with another. Both halves are indicative, present or past. A **future more vivid** condition talks about a future that is quite likely. Both halves look to the future, so Latin uses the **future indicative**, or the **future perfect** in the protasis when that action must be finished first.'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Sī **pluit**, domī **manēmus**.",en:"If it is raining, we stay at home.",note:"Simple present."},{la:"Sī id **dīxit**, **errāvit**.",en:"If he said that, he was wrong.",note:"Simple past."},{la:"Sī mē **vocābis**, **veniam**.",en:"If you call me, I will come.",note:"Future more vivid."},{la:"Sī epistulam **scrīpseris**, eam **mittam**.",en:"If you write the letter, I will send it.",note:"Future perfect in the protasis: the writing comes first."}]},{kind:"p",text:'English says "if you call" with a present tense, but Latin is exact: the calling is in the future, so it says vocābis, "you will call".'}]},{heading:'Future Less Vivid: "should... would"',blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'The **future less vivid** condition imagines a future that is possible but less likely. It uses the **present subjunctive in both halves**. Translate with "should" (or "were to") in the protasis and "would" in the apodosis.'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Sī rēx **redeat**, cīvēs **gaudeant**.",en:"If the king should return, the citizens would rejoice."},{la:"Sī hostēs **appropinquent**, portās **claudāmus**.",en:"If the enemy should approach, we would close the gates."}]},{kind:"p",text:'Compare the future more vivid *Sī rēx redībit, cīvēs gaudēbunt*, "If the king returns, the citizens will rejoice." The meaning is close; the subjunctive simply makes the event sound less certain.'}]},{heading:'Present CTF: "were... would"',blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'A **present contrary-to-fact** (CTF) condition imagines something that is **not true now**. It uses the **imperfect subjunctive in both halves**. Translate "If ... were ..., ... would ...".'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Sī dīves **essem**, vīllam magnam **emerem**.",en:"If I were rich, I would buy a large country house.",note:"I am not rich."},{la:"Sī frāter meus **adesset**, nōs **adiuvāret**.",en:"If my brother were here, he would be helping us."}]},{kind:"p",text:'Do not be misled by the name "imperfect". In this construction the imperfect subjunctive refers to the **present**.'}]},{heading:"Past CTF and mixed conditions",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'A **past contrary-to-fact** condition imagines something that **did not happen** in the past. It uses the **pluperfect subjunctive in both halves**. Translate "If ... had ..., ... would have ...".'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Sī mātūrius **profectī essēmus**, ante noctem **pervēnissēmus**.",en:"If we had set out earlier, we would have arrived before night."},{la:"Nisi mīlitēs fortiter **pugnāvissent**, urbs **capta esset**.",en:"If the soldiers had not fought bravely, the city would have been captured."}]},{kind:"p",text:'In a **mixed** contrary-to-fact condition, the two halves refer to different times. Most often a past condition (**pluperfect** subjunctive) has a present result (**imperfect** subjunctive). Words like nunc ("now"), hodiē ("today") and herī ("yesterday") help you see the change of time.'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Sī mātūrius **profectī essēmus**, nunc domī **essēmus**.",en:"If we had set out earlier, we would be at home now."}]}]},{heading:"Naming a conditional",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:"Check the mood first. Indicative in both halves means a simple or future more vivid condition: look at the tense to choose. Subjunctive means one of the less vivid or contrary-to-fact types, and the tense tells you which: **present** = future less vivid, **imperfect** = present CTF, **pluperfect** = past CTF."},{kind:"tip",title:"Name each half by its own tense",text:'Do not assume both halves match. If the protasis is pluperfect subjunctive ("had...") and the apodosis is imperfect subjunctive ("would... now"), it is a **mixed** condition, not a past CTF. And do not mix up present subjunctive with imperfect: *sī veniat* is "if he should come" (future less vivid), but *sī venīret* is "if he were coming" (present CTF).'},{kind:"tip",title:"Future more vivid uses the future",text:'In a future more vivid condition both halves are **future indicative** (or future perfect in the protasis), even though English puts the "if" clause in the present.'}]}],check:["m7-102","m7-201","m7-302","m7-501","m7-505"]},o={moduleId:"cum_clauses",title:"Cum Clauses",summary:'With a subjunctive, cum means "when", "since" or "although". Learn to tell the preposition from the conjunction, and the three subjunctive uses from one another and from the indicative temporal cum.',sections:[{heading:"Preposition or conjunction?",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'The word cum does two different jobs. As a **preposition** it takes the **ablative** and means "with". As a **conjunction** it introduces a whole clause with its own verb, and then it means "when", "since" or "although".'},{kind:"p",text:'So before you translate cum, look at what follows it. If an ablative noun or pronoun comes straight after it and there is no verb in the phrase, cum means "with". If cum starts a clause with a verb, it is a conjunction, and the verb is usually **subjunctive**.'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"**Cum sorōre** in hortō sedēbam.",en:"I was sitting in the garden with my sister.",note:"sorōre is ablative and there is no verb with cum: preposition."},{la:"**Cum** nāvis portuī **appropinquāret**, nautae gaudēbant.",en:"When the ship was approaching the harbour, the sailors were rejoicing.",note:"appropinquāret is a subjunctive verb: conjunction."}]},{kind:"p",text:"A cum clause with the subjunctive follows the **sequence of tenses**. After a past main verb, the imperfect subjunctive shows the **same time** and the pluperfect shows **time before**. After a present or future main verb, the present and perfect do the same jobs."}]},{heading:'Circumstantial Cum: "when"',blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'A **circumstantial** cum clause describes the situation in which a past event happened. It uses the **imperfect** subjunctive for background that was going on at the time ("when / while ... was ...ing") and the **pluperfect** subjunctive for something already finished ("when / after ... had ..."). Translate cum as "when".'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"**Cum** per silvam **errārent**, lupum vīdērunt.",en:"When they were wandering through the forest, they saw a wolf.",note:"Imperfect: the wandering was going on when they saw the wolf."},{la:"**Cum** Hannibal Alpēs **trānsiisset**, in Italiam dēscendit.",en:"When Hannibal had crossed the Alps, he came down into Italy.",note:"Pluperfect: the crossing was over first."},{la:"**Cum** epistula **lēcta esset**, omnēs tacuērunt.",en:"When the letter had been read, everyone fell silent."}]},{kind:"p",text:'English usually says "when he heard" where Latin says cum audīvisset, "when he had heard". Both are fine translations; the Latin pluperfect simply makes clear that the hearing came first.'}]},{heading:'Causal Cum: "since"',blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'A **causal** cum clause gives the **reason** for the main action. Translate cum as "since" or "because". Unlike the circumstantial use, it is not limited to the past: it can stand in either sequence and use any of the four subjunctive tenses.'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"**Cum** via longa **sit**, hodiē nōn proficīscēmur.",en:"Since the road is long, we will not set out today.",note:"Future main verb: primary sequence, present subjunctive."},{la:"**Cum** pater **discesserit**, nōs domum cūrāmus.",en:"Since Father has left, we are looking after the house.",note:"Primary sequence, time before: perfect subjunctive."},{la:"**Cum** frūmentum nōn **habērent**, oppidum relīquērunt.",en:"Since they had no grain, they left the town."}]},{kind:"p",text:'Sometimes a past cum clause makes sense both as a background ("when") and as a reason ("since"). Then either translation is reasonable. Let the meaning of the two clauses guide you.'}]},{heading:'Concessive Cum: "although"',blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'A **concessive** cum clause states a fact that you would expect to stop the main action, but it does not. Translate cum as "although". The main clause very often contains **tamen** ("nevertheless"), and when it does, the cum clause is almost certainly concessive.'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"**Cum** mīlitēs fessī **essent**, **tamen** iter fēcērunt.",en:"Although the soldiers were tired, they nevertheless kept marching."},{la:"**Cum** tē **amem**, hoc tibi dare nōn possum.",en:"Although I love you, I cannot give you this.",note:"No tamen here: the clash between the two clauses shows it is concessive."}]}]},{heading:"Temporal Cum with the indicative",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'Not every cum clause is subjunctive. When cum refers to **present or future time** and simply says "when", it takes the **indicative**. Notice that Latin uses a future where English uses a present.'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"**Cum** Rōmam **veniēs**, mē vidēbis.",en:"When you come to Rome, you will see me."}]},{kind:"p",text:'In the past, cum takes the indicative when it only dates an event ("at the time when"), and in **cum inversum**. In cum inversum the order of ideas is turned around: the first clause sets a scene in the imperfect or pluperfect, often with iam or vix, and the cum clause reports the main, sudden event in the indicative, often with subitō.'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Iam nox erat, **cum** nūntius subitō **advēnit**.",en:"It was already night when suddenly a messenger arrived."}]}]},{heading:"Telling them apart",blocks:[{kind:"table",head:["Use","Mood","English","Clue"],rows:[["cum + ablative","no verb","with","an ablative noun right after cum"],["Circumstantial","imperfect or pluperfect subjunctive","when","background to a past event"],["Causal","subjunctive, any tense","since","the clause gives a reason"],["Concessive","subjunctive, any tense","although","tamen, or a clash with the main clause"],["Temporal","indicative","when","present or future time; cum inversum"]]},{kind:"tip",title:"Check for a verb before you translate cum",text:'The commonest slip is translating every cum as "when". First ask: is there a verb in the cum phrase? If not, it is the preposition "with": *cum amīcō*, "with a friend". If there is, check its mood. An indicative means plain temporal "when" (*cum veniēs*). A subjunctive means circumstantial, causal or concessive, and the main clause tells you which.'},{kind:"tip",title:"Imperfect for background",text:'In a circumstantial clause, the **imperfect** subjunctive shows something going on at the same time as the past main verb. The **pluperfect** shows something already finished. Do not read the imperfect as "had".'}]}],check:["m4-103","m4-202","m4-203","m4-409","m4-504"]},r={moduleId:"fear_clauses",title:"Fear Clauses",summary:'After a verb of fearing, nē means "that" and ut means "that ... not". Learn the verbs that set up a fear clause, the reversal of the two conjunctions, and how tense works.',sections:[{heading:"Verbs of fearing",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:"A fear clause says what someone is afraid **might happen** (or might have happened). It depends on a verb or expression of fearing, and its verb is **subjunctive**."},{kind:"table",head:["Latin","Meaning"],rows:[["timeō, timēre","fear, be afraid"],["metuō, metuere","fear, be afraid"],["vereor, verērī (deponent)","fear, be afraid"],["timor est, metus est","there is fear"],["perīculum est","there is a danger"]]},{kind:"p",text:'Do not confuse these with lookalike verbs that have nothing to do with fear: **vetō** ("forbid"), **volō** ("want") and **vertō** ("turn").'}]},{heading:'Positive Fear: nē = "that"',blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'When you fear that something **will** happen, Latin uses **nē**. Translate it as **"that"** (older English uses "lest"). This feels backwards, because elsewhere nē is negative. It makes sense if you think of the fear clause as a wish: "I am afraid: may he not come!" means "I am afraid **that** he will come."'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Timeō **nē** frāter meus **aegrōtet**.",en:"I am afraid that my brother is ill."},{la:"Nautae metuēbant **nē** tempestās nāvem **dēlēret**.",en:"The sailors were afraid that the storm would destroy the ship."},{la:"Vereor **nē** hoc frūstrā **fēcerīmus**.",en:"I am afraid that we have done this for nothing."},{la:"Perīculum erat **nē** hostēs oppidum **caperent**.",en:"There was a danger that the enemy would capture the town."}]}]},{heading:'Negative Fear: ut or nē nōn = "that ... not"',blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'When you fear that something will **not** happen, Latin uses **ut** or **nē nōn**. Both mean **"that ... not"**. The verb itself is an ordinary subjunctive; the "not" comes from the conjunction.'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Timeō **ut** epistula ad tē **perveniat**.",en:"I am afraid that the letter will not reach you."},{la:"Verēbāmur **nē** frūmentum **nōn sufficeret**.",en:"We were afraid that the grain would not be enough."}]},{kind:"tip",title:"The reversal",text:'In a fear clause the conjunctions swap their usual meanings. **nē** means **"that"**: *Timeō nē nāvis mergātur*, "I am afraid that the ship will sink." **ut** (or **nē nōn**) means **"that ... not"**: *Timeō ut nāvis redeat*, "I am afraid that the ship will not come back." Never translate ut here as "so that" or "in order to", and never add a "not" for nē.'}]},{heading:"Tense in a fear clause",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'Fear clauses follow the sequence of tenses. Because fears usually look **ahead**, the present subjunctive (primary) and the imperfect subjunctive (secondary) cover both what is happening now and what may happen **later**. English often needs "will" or "would" for them. The perfect and pluperfect show a fear about something that may **already** have happened.'},{kind:"table",caption:'vincō in a fear clause: "that the enemy ..."',head:["Main verb","Same time or later","Time before"],rows:[["Primary: Timeō nē hostēs ...",'vincant: "will win, are winning"','vīcerint: "have won"'],["Secondary: Timēbam nē hostēs ...",'vincerent: "would win, were winning"','vīcissent: "had won"']]},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Metuimus **nē** pōns **frangātur**.",en:"We are afraid that the bridge will be broken.",note:"Primary, looking ahead: present subjunctive."},{la:"Verita est **nē** fīlius **āmissus esset**.",en:"She was afraid that her son had been lost.",note:"Secondary, time before: pluperfect subjunctive."}]}]},{heading:"Fear clause or something else?",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'The same conjunctions appear in purpose clauses, where nē means "so that ... not". Look at the main verb. If it is a verb of fearing, nē means "that". If the clause gives the aim of an action, nē means "so that ... not".'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Portam claudimus **nē** lupī **intrent**.",en:"We are shutting the gate so that the wolves may not come in.",note:"Purpose clause."},{la:"Timēmus **nē** lupī **intrent**.",en:"We are afraid that the wolves will come in.",note:"Fear clause."}]},{kind:"p",text:'A verb of fearing can also take a plain **infinitive**, meaning "be afraid **to** do something". Then there is no conjunction and no subjunctive.'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Puer **timet** in aquam **dēsilīre**.",en:"The boy is afraid to jump into the water."}]},{kind:"table",head:["Meaning","Latin","English"],rows:[["fear that something will happen","nē + subjunctive","that ..."],["fear that something will not happen","ut or nē nōn + subjunctive","that ... not"],["be afraid to do something yourself","infinitive","to ..."]]}]}],check:["m6-102","m6-201","m6-204","m6-302","m6-607"]},u={moduleId:"forms_bootcamp",title:"The Four Subjunctive Tenses",summary:"The subjunctive has four tenses: present, imperfect, perfect and pluperfect. Learn to build each one in the active and the passive, and to tell a subjunctive from an indicative on sight.",sections:[{heading:"Present Active Subjunctive",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:"Every subjunctive in the later modules uses one of four tenses: present, imperfect, perfect or pluperfect. There is no future subjunctive. A subjunctive has no single English meaning; how you translate it depends on the construction it sits in. Here you only learn to build and recognise the forms."},{kind:"p",text:"To make the present active subjunctive, change the vowel in front of the personal ending, then add the active endings **-m, -s, -t, -mus, -tis, -nt**. The first person singular ends in **-m**, not -ō."},{kind:"table",caption:"Present active subjunctive",head:["Person","amō (1st)","moneō (2nd)","regō (3rd)","audiō (4th)"],rows:[["1st sg.","amem","moneam","regam","audiam"],["2nd sg.","amēs","moneās","regās","audiās"],["3rd sg.","amet","moneat","regat","audiat"],["1st pl.","amēmus","moneāmus","regāmus","audiāmus"],["2nd pl.","amētis","moneātis","regātis","audiātis"],["3rd pl.","ament","moneant","regant","audiant"]]},{kind:"tip",title:"We fear a liar",text:"The vowels run *wE fEAr A lIAr*: **e** in the 1st conjugation (amem), **ea** in the 2nd (moneam), **a** in the 3rd (regam), **ia** in the 3rd -iō and the 4th (capiam, audiam). Notice that the 1st conjugation swaps its ā for e, while every other conjugation brings in an a."},{kind:"p",text:"The vowel is long in most forms (amēs, regāmus) but short before a final -m or -t and before -nt (amem, amet, ament). Verbs like capiō, capere work exactly like audiō: capiam, capiās, capiat, capiāmus, capiātis, capiant."},{kind:"p",text:"*esse* and *posse* are irregular and must be memorised. They use the vowel i."},{kind:"table",caption:"Present subjunctive of esse and posse",head:["Person","esse","posse"],rows:[["1st sg.","sim","possim"],["2nd sg.","sīs","possīs"],["3rd sg.","sit","possit"],["1st pl.","sīmus","possīmus"],["2nd pl.","sītis","possītis"],["3rd pl.","sint","possint"]]}]},{heading:"Present Passive Subjunctive",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:"Keep the same vowel and swap in the passive endings **-r, -ris, -tur, -mur, -minī, -ntur**. If you can say amem, you can say amer."},{kind:"table",caption:"Present passive subjunctive",head:["Person","amō (1st)","moneō (2nd)","regō (3rd)","audiō (4th)"],rows:[["1st sg.","amer","monear","regar","audiar"],["2nd sg.","amēris","moneāris","regāris","audiāris"],["3rd sg.","amētur","moneātur","regātur","audiātur"],["1st pl.","amēmur","moneāmur","regāmur","audiāmur"],["2nd pl.","amēminī","moneāminī","regāminī","audiāminī"],["3rd pl.","amentur","moneantur","regantur","audiantur"]]},{kind:"p",text:"The vowel is short before the final -r and before -ntur (amer, amentur). Deponent verbs use these same passive forms with an active meaning: loquor gives loquar, loquāris, loquātur."}]},{heading:"Imperfect Active and Imperfect Passive Subjunctive",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:"This is the easiest tense. Take the **present active infinitive** (the second principal part) and add the endings straight onto it: active -m, -s, -t, -mus, -tis, -nt, or passive -r, -ris, -tur, -mur, -minī, -ntur. The final e of the infinitive becomes long except before -m, -t, -nt and a final -r."},{kind:"table",caption:"Imperfect subjunctive of amō (infinitive amāre)",head:["Person","Active","Passive"],rows:[["1st sg.","amārem","amārer"],["2nd sg.","amārēs","amārēris"],["3rd sg.","amāret","amārētur"],["1st pl.","amārēmus","amārēmur"],["2nd pl.","amārētis","amārēminī"],["3rd pl.","amārent","amārentur"]]},{kind:"table",caption:"The rule works for every conjugation, and even for esse and posse",head:["Infinitive","Imperfect subjunctive, 1st sg."],rows:[["amāre","amārem"],["monēre","monērem"],["regere","regerem"],["capere","caperem"],["audīre","audīrem"],["esse","essem"],["posse","possem"]]},{kind:"tip",title:"Use the real infinitive",text:'capiō has the infinitive **capere**, so its imperfect subjunctive is **caperem**, not "capirem". Always build from the infinitive itself, never from the first principal part.'}]},{heading:"Perfect Active and Perfect Passive Subjunctive",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:"**Active:** take the perfect stem (the third principal part without its -ī: amāvī gives amāv-) and add **-erim, -erīs, -erit, -erīmus, -erītis, -erint**. So dūcō gives dūxerim, moneō gives monuerim, audiō gives audīverim, capiō gives cēperim."},{kind:"p",text:"**Passive:** take the perfect passive participle (from the fourth principal part: amātum gives amātus) and add the **present subjunctive of esse**: sim, sīs, sit, sīmus, sītis, sint. The participle agrees with the subject, so it can be amātus, amāta or amātum in the singular and amātī, amātae or amāta in the plural."},{kind:"table",caption:"Perfect subjunctive of amō",head:["Person","Active","Passive"],rows:[["1st sg.","amāverim","amātus sim"],["2nd sg.","amāverīs","amātus sīs"],["3rd sg.","amāverit","amātus sit"],["1st pl.","amāverīmus","amātī sīmus"],["2nd pl.","amāverītis","amātī sītis"],["3rd pl.","amāverint","amātī sint"]]}]},{heading:"Pluperfect Active and Pluperfect Passive Subjunctive",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:"**Active:** perfect stem + **-issem, -issēs, -isset, -issēmus, -issētis, -issent**. A handy shortcut: it looks like the perfect active infinitive (amāvisse) with the endings added."},{kind:"p",text:"**Passive:** perfect passive participle + the **imperfect subjunctive of esse**: essem, essēs, esset, essēmus, essētis, essent."},{kind:"table",caption:"Pluperfect subjunctive of amō",head:["Person","Active","Passive"],rows:[["1st sg.","amāvissem","amātus essem"],["2nd sg.","amāvissēs","amātus essēs"],["3rd sg.","amāvisset","amātus esset"],["1st pl.","amāvissēmus","amātī essēmus"],["2nd pl.","amāvissētis","amātī essētis"],["3rd pl.","amāvissent","amātī essent"]]},{kind:"tip",title:"Two-word passives",text:'In a two-word passive, the little form of esse decides the tense. **amātus sit** (present subjunctive sit) is perfect passive subjunctive. **amātus esset** (imperfect subjunctive esset) is pluperfect passive subjunctive. Do not call amātus esset "imperfect" just because esset is imperfect.'}]},{heading:"Indicative vs. Subjunctive recognition",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:"To spot a subjunctive, look at what sits between the stem and the ending. Each tense has its own signal."},{kind:"table",head:["Tense","Signal","Subjunctive","Indicative look-alike"],rows:[["Present","vowel change (e, ea, a, ia)","amet, regat","amat, regit"],["Imperfect","whole infinitive + ending","amāret","amābat"],["Perfect","perfect stem + -eri-","amāverim","amāvī, amāverō"],["Pluperfect","perfect stem + -isse-","amāvisset","amāverat"],["Perfect passive","participle + sim, sit...","amātus sit","amātus est"],["Pluperfect passive","participle + essem, esset...","amātus esset","amātus erat"]]},{kind:"p",text:"**-ba-** (or -bā-) right before the personal ending marks the imperfect indicative (amābat, regēbat, audiēbātur). If the infinitive is sitting inside the verb instead (amāret, regeret), it is the imperfect subjunctive."},{kind:"tip",title:"Three look-alikes to watch",text:"In the 3rd conjugation, **a** marks the present subjunctive and **e** marks the future: dūcāmur is subjunctive, dūcēmur is future. But the first person singular is the same in both (regam, capiam, audiam can be either), so let the sentence decide. Finally, apart from the long ī in some forms (dūxerīs vs dūxeris), the perfect subjunctive and the future perfect indicative are spelled the same except in the first person singular (dūxerim vs dūxerō). A form in -erim can only be perfect subjunctive. On its own, dūxerint could be either; the sentence decides."}]}],check:["f003","f040","f044","f051","f062"]},h={moduleId:"gerund_gerundive",title:"Gerund and Gerundive",summary:'The gerund is a verbal noun ("of doing", "by doing"); the gerundive is a verbal adjective ("to be done"). Both have -nd- in the stem. Together they express purpose, and the gerundive with sum expresses obligation.',sections:[{heading:"Gerund: a Verbal Noun",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'The **gerund** is a noun made from a verb, like English "reading" in "the art of reading". It is a **neuter singular** noun of the 2nd declension, formed from the present stem + **-nd-**: -andī in the 1st conjugation, -endī in the 2nd and 3rd, -iendī in 3rd -iō and 4th. It is active in meaning, and deponents form it in the same way (hortandī, "of encouraging"; loquendī, "of speaking").'},{kind:"table",caption:"The gerund",head:["Case","laudō","dūcō","capiō","audiō"],rows:[["Nominative","none: use laudāre","none: use dūcere","none: use capere","none: use audīre"],["Genitive","laudandī","dūcendī","capiendī","audiendī"],["Dative","laudandō","dūcendō","capiendō","audiendō"],["Accusative","(ad) laudandum","(ad) dūcendum","(ad) capiendum","(ad) audiendum"],["Ablative","laudandō","dūcendō","capiendō","audiendō"]]},{kind:"p",text:'The gerund has **no nominative**. When a verbal noun is the subject, Latin uses the **infinitive**: *Nāvigāre perīculōsum est*, "Sailing is dangerous." The accusative gerund is used mostly after a preposition such as **ad**. The genitive goes with nouns and with adjectives like cupidus and perītus; the dative is rare; the ablative usually shows **means**.'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Iuvenis **pugnandī** cupidus erat.",en:"The young man was eager to fight (eager for fighting)."},{la:"Haec silva **vēnandō** apta est.",en:"This wood is suitable for hunting.",note:"Dative."},{la:"Puerī in campum ad **lūdendum** cucurrērunt.",en:"The boys ran onto the field to play."},{la:"Fabrī **labōrandō** dīvitēs factī sunt.",en:"The craftsmen became rich by working.",note:"Ablative of means."}]}]},{heading:"Gerundive: a Verbal Adjective",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'The **gerundive** has the same -nd- stem, but it is an **adjective**: -ndus, -nda, -ndum, declined like bonus. It **agrees with a noun**, and it is **passive**: it means "to be ...ed", often with the idea of something that ought to be done.'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Hic est vir **laudandus**.",en:"This is a man to be praised (a man who deserves praise)."},{la:"Caesar pontem in flūmine **faciendum** cūrāvit.",en:"Caesar had a bridge built over the river.",note:'Literally "saw to a bridge to be built".'}]},{kind:"p",text:'Do not confuse the -nd- forms with the **present participle**, which has **-nt-** (laudantem, "praising"). A gerund never agrees with anything; a gerundive always does.'}]},{heading:"Gerundive in Place of Gerund + Object",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'A gerund can in theory take a direct object (urbem videndī, "of seeing the city"), but Latin usually prefers the **gerundive construction**. Put the noun into the case the gerund would have had, and make the gerundive **agree** with it. After a preposition such as ad, the gerundive is the regular choice and the gerund + object is avoided.'},{kind:"table",head:["Case","Gerund + object","Gerundive construction","English"],rows:[["Genitive","urbem videndī","urbis videndae","of seeing the city"],["Accusative (ad)","ad nāvēs reficiendum (avoided)","ad nāvēs reficiendās","to repair the ships"],["Ablative","librōs legendō","librīs legendīs","by reading books"]]},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Mīlitēs ad **nāvēs reficiendās** missī sunt.",en:"The soldiers were sent to repair the ships.",note:"reficiendās agrees with nāvēs: feminine accusative plural."},{la:"Ingenium **librīs legendīs** alitur.",en:"The mind is nourished by reading books."}]},{kind:"p",text:'English translates the gerundive construction exactly like the gerund: "of seeing the city", not "of the city to be seen".'}]},{heading:"Purpose: ad + Gerund or Gerundive; causā or grātiā + Genitive",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'Classical prose does not use a plain infinitive to show purpose. Besides an ut clause, it can use **ad** + accusative gerund or gerundive, or a genitive gerund or gerundive + **causā** or **grātiā**, "for the sake of". causā and grātiā come **after** the genitive. All of these are usually translated "to ..." or "in order to ...".'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Senātōrēs ad **rem pūblicam dēfendendam** convēnērunt.",en:"The senators gathered to defend the state."},{la:"Multī Rōmam **discendī causā** veniunt.",en:"Many people come to Rome to learn."},{la:"Caesar equitēs **locī cognōscendī causā** praemīsit.",en:"Caesar sent the cavalry ahead to explore the area."},{la:"Mercātōrēs **pecūniae faciendae grātiā** nāvigābant.",en:"The merchants sailed to make money."}]}]},{heading:"Passive Periphrastic (Obligation) and the Dative of Agent",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'The gerundive + a form of **sum** is the **passive periphrastic**. It means "**must be ...ed**", "has to be ...ed". The tense of sum sets the time: est, "must"; erat, "had to"; erit, "will have to". The gerundive agrees with the subject.'},{kind:"p",text:'The person who must do the action is in the **dative**, not ā/ab + ablative. This is the **dative of agent**. English often sounds better with an active verb: "by us must be done" becomes "we must do".'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Hic mūrus **reficiendus est**.",en:"This wall must be repaired."},{la:"**Puerīs** librī **legendī erant**.",en:"The books had to be read by the boys. (The boys had to read the books.)"},{la:"**Tibi** haec rēs **cōnsīderanda erit**.",en:"This matter will have to be considered by you. (You will have to consider this matter.)"}]}]},{heading:"Impersonal Passive Periphrastic",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'A verb with **no direct object** (currō, pugnō, veniō) cannot have a personal passive subject. Latin then uses the **neuter singular** gerundive -ndum with est, erat or erit and no subject: "it must be fought" means "one must fight". Add a dative of agent to say who must do it.'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Nōbīs mox **discēdendum est**.",en:"We must leave soon."},{la:"Mīlitibus tōtam noctem **vigilandum erit**.",en:"The soldiers will have to stay awake all night."}]},{kind:"tip",title:"Dative of agent only with gerundive + sum",text:'With an ordinary passive verb the doer is **ā/ab + ablative**: *Urbs ā Gallīs capta est*, "The city was captured by the Gauls." The **dative** of agent goes with the **passive periphrastic**: *Urbs nōbīs dēfendenda est*, "The city must be defended by us." And a gerundive means "**to be** ...ed", never "being ...ed": *vir laudandus*, "a man to be praised".'}]}],check:["m12-202","m12-304","m12-402","m12-406","m12-601"]},d={moduleId:"independent_uses",title:"Independent Subjunctives",summary:"Four uses of the subjunctive stand alone as the main verb of a sentence: hortatory, jussive, optative and deliberative. Learn what each one says, how to translate it, and which negative it takes.",sections:[{heading:"A subjunctive on its own",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:"In most sentences the subjunctive sits in a dependent clause. In these four uses it is the **main verb**. There is no ut and no main verb to follow, so you identify the use from three clues: the **person** of the verb, its **tense**, and whether the sentence is a **question** or contains **utinam**."},{kind:"p",text:"The negative is part of the pattern too. Hortatory, jussive and optative subjunctives use **nē**. Only the deliberative uses **nōn**."}]},{heading:'Hortatory: "Let us..."',blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'The hortatory subjunctive urges a group that includes the speaker to act. It is a **1st person** (almost always plural) **present subjunctive**, translated "Let us...". The negative is **nē**: "Let us not...".'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"**Cantēmus** et **rīdeāmus**!",en:"Let us sing and laugh!"},{la:"**Scrībāmus** epistulam.",en:"Let us write a letter."},{la:"**Nē timeāmus** hostēs.",en:"Let us not fear the enemy."}]}]},{heading:'Jussive: "Let him...", "Let them..."',blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'The jussive subjunctive gives an order to someone who is not being spoken to directly. It is a **3rd person present subjunctive**, translated "Let him / her / it / them..." or "He / They should...". It can be passive. The negative is **nē**.'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"**Taceat** puer!",en:"Let the boy be quiet!"},{la:"Servī **labōrent**.",en:"Let the slaves work."},{la:"Porta **claudātur**.",en:"Let the gate be shut."},{la:"**Nē** mīlitēs **dormiant**.",en:"Let the soldiers not sleep."}]},{kind:"p",text:"To forbid the person you are talking to (2nd person), Latin uses **nē + perfect subjunctive**. This is called the **prohibitive** subjunctive. Another common way is **nōlī** (to one person) or **nōlīte** (to several) + infinitive."},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"**Nē** hoc **dīxerīs**!",en:"Do not say this!"},{la:"**Nē** mē **relīquerītis**!",en:"Do not leave me!"},{la:"**Nōlīte currere**!",en:"Do not run!"}]},{kind:"tip",title:"A perfect that is not past",text:'In a prohibition, the perfect subjunctive has no past meaning at all. **Nē hoc dīxerīs** means "Do not say this!", not "You did not say this" or "May you not have said this".'}]},{heading:"Optative: wishes",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'The optative subjunctive expresses a wish. It is often introduced by **utinam** ("if only", "would that"), and its negative is **nē** (utinam nē). The tense tells you what kind of wish it is.'},{kind:"table",head:["Tense","Kind of wish","Example","English"],rows:[["present","possible, for the future","Utinam fīlius meus salvus **redeat**!","May my son come home safe!"],["imperfect","impossible now (unfulfilled present)","Utinam pater nōs **spectāret**!","If only Father were watching us!"],["pluperfect","impossible in the past (unfulfilled past)","Utinam domī **mānsissēs**!","If only you had stayed at home!"]]},{kind:"p",text:"The imperfect and pluperfect here match the tenses of contrary-to-fact conditions: imperfect for something untrue now, pluperfect for something that did not happen in the past. A present wish can also stand without utinam."},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Utinam **nē** illud **dīxissem**!",en:"If only I had not said that!"},{la:"**Valeās**!",en:"May you be well! (Farewell!)"}]}]},{heading:'Deliberative: "What am I to do?"',blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'The deliberative subjunctive is a **question** in which the speaker wonders what to do. Often it is not a real request for information but a way of thinking aloud. Use the **present subjunctive** for doubt now ("What am I to...?", "What should I...?") and the **imperfect subjunctive** for doubt in the past ("What was I to...?"). The negative is **nōn**.'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Quem **vocem**?",en:"Whom am I to call?"},{la:"Ubi **dormiāmus**?",en:"Where are we to sleep?"},{la:"Quō **fugerem**?",en:"Where was I to flee?"},{la:"Cūr **nōn ambulēmus**?",en:"Why should we not take a walk?"}]}]},{heading:"Telling them apart",blocks:[{kind:"table",head:["Use","Look for","English","Negative"],rows:[["Hortatory","1st person plural, present","Let us...","nē"],["Jussive","3rd person, present","Let him... / Let them...","nē"],["Prohibitive","2nd person, perfect, with nē","Do not...!","nē"],["Optative","a wish, often with utinam; present, imperfect or pluperfect","May... / If only...","nē"],["Deliberative","a question; present or imperfect","What am I to...? / What was I to...?","nōn"]]},{kind:"tip",title:"nē or nōn?",text:"Exhortations, commands, prohibitions and wishes all take **nē**. The deliberative is the only one that takes **nōn**: *Cūr nōn ambulēmus?*, never nē. If a subjunctive main verb sits in a question, think deliberative and nōn."}]}],check:["m2-202","m2-306","m2-403","m2-505","m2-601"]},l={moduleId:"indirect_question",title:"Indirect Question",summary:"A question reported after a verb of asking, knowing or telling becomes an indirect question. It keeps its question word, puts its verb in the subjunctive, and follows the sequence of tenses strictly.",sections:[{heading:"From direct to indirect",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:`A direct question is asked out loud: "Who came?" An **indirect question** reports a question inside another sentence: "I ask **who came**", "I don't know **who came**". In Latin the reported question keeps its **question word**, and its verb becomes **subjunctive**.`},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Quis vēnit?",en:"Who came?",note:"Direct question: indicative."},{la:"Rogō **quis vēnerit**.",en:"I ask who came.",note:"Indirect question: subjunctive."}]},{kind:"p",text:'The main verb is a verb of **asking, knowing, telling, wondering, seeing or finding out**: rogō, quaerō, sciō, nesciō, intellegō, mīror, cognōscō, videō, and the command dīc mihi ("tell me"). Translate the subjunctive as a plain English verb: "who came", not "who might come".'}]},{heading:"Interrogatives: the question words",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:"An indirect question always begins with a **question word**. Learn these well, because they are the signal that tells you what kind of clause you are reading."},{kind:"table",head:["Latin","Meaning"],rows:[["quis, quid","who, what"],["quī, quae, quod","which, what (with a noun)"],["cūr","why"],["ubi","where (at what place)"],["quō","where to (to what place)"],["unde","where from (from what place)"],["quandō","when"],["quōmodo","how, in what way"],["quantus, -a, -um","how great, how large"],["quālis, -e","what kind of"],["quot","how many (does not decline)"],["num, -ne","whether"],["utrum ... an","whether ... or"],["utrum ... necne","whether ... or not"]]},{kind:"p",text:"Notice that **ubi**, **quō** and **unde** are not interchangeable. Latin separates being at a place (ubi), moving to it (quō) and moving away from it (unde)."},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Nesciō **ubi** frāter meus **habitet**.",en:"I do not know where my brother lives."},{la:"Rogāvit **num** fessī **essēmus**.",en:"He asked whether we were tired."},{la:"Dīc mihi **utrum** Rōmam **eās an** domī **maneās**.",en:"Tell me whether you are going to Rome or staying at home."},{la:"Nūntius nōn dīxit **quot** hostēs **adessent**.",en:"The messenger did not say how many enemies were present."}]},{kind:"tip",title:'num means "whether"',text:'In a direct question, num expects the answer "no" ("Surely ... not?"). In an indirect question it loses that meaning and simply means **"whether"**: *Rogāvit num fessī essēmus*, "He asked whether we were tired."'}]},{heading:"Indirect question or relative clause?",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:"Question words and relative pronouns can look alike (quis, quī, quod). A **relative clause** describes a noun in the main clause (its antecedent) and usually has an **indicative** verb. An **indirect question** has no antecedent: it is the object of a verb of asking or knowing, and its verb is **subjunctive**."},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Cīvem vīdī **quī** epistulam **portābat**.",en:"I saw the citizen who was carrying the letter.",note:"quī refers back to cīvem; indicative: relative clause."},{la:"Rogāvī **quis** epistulam **portāret**.",en:"I asked who was carrying the letter.",note:"No antecedent, verb of asking, subjunctive: indirect question."}]},{kind:"p",text:'Watch rogō in particular. With **ut** or **nē** it introduces an indirect command ("ask someone to do something"). With a question word it introduces an indirect question ("ask who, why, whether").'}]},{heading:"SOT Alignment: choosing the tense",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:"Indirect questions follow the sequence of tenses strictly, and unlike purpose clauses they use **all four** subjunctive tenses, because a question can ask about something going on now or something that already happened. First find the sequence from the main verb, then ask whether the action in the question is at the **same time** as the main verb or **before** it."},{kind:"table",caption:'legō in an indirect question, "what you ..."',head:["Sequence","Same time","Time before"],rows:[["Primary: Rogat quid ...",'legās: "what you are reading"','lēgerīs: "what you read / have read"'],["Secondary: Rogāvit quid ...",'legerēs: "what you were reading"','lēgissēs: "what you had read"']]},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Intellegō cūr puellae **rīdeant**.",en:"I understand why the girls are laughing.",note:"Primary, same time: present subjunctive."},{la:"Nescīmus quō hostēs **fūgerint**.",en:"We do not know where the enemy fled to.",note:"Primary, time before: perfect subjunctive."},{la:"Cognōvit unde nāvēs **vēnissent**.",en:"He found out where the ships had come from.",note:"Secondary, time before: pluperfect subjunctive."},{la:"Quaerēbant quis mūrum **dēlēvisset**.",en:"They were asking who had destroyed the wall."}]},{kind:"tip",title:"Present or perfect?",text:'After a primary main verb, the present subjunctive means "is doing" and the perfect subjunctive means "did" or "has done". *Rogat quid legās* is "He asks what you are reading"; *Rogat quid lēgerīs* is "He asks what you read". Do not translate the perfect subjunctive as a present.'}]},{heading:"Questions about the future",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:"The subjunctive has no future tense. When an indirect question looks ahead, Latin uses the future active participle (-ūrus) with **sim** in primary sequence or **essem** in secondary sequence."},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Nesciō quid **factūrus sīs**.",en:"I do not know what you will do."},{la:"Rogāvērunt quandō **reditūrī essēmus**.",en:"They asked when we would return."}]}]}],check:["m5-103","m5-203","m5-303","m5-404","m5-603"]},c={moduleId:"indirect_statement",title:"Indirect Statement",summary:"After a verb of saying, thinking, knowing or perceiving, Latin reports a statement with an accusative subject and an infinitive. The tense of the infinitive shows time relative to the main verb.",sections:[{heading:"Verbs of Saying, Thinking, Knowing and Perceiving",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:`A direct statement gives someone's exact words: "The enemy are coming." An **indirect statement** reports them: "He says **that** the enemy are coming." In Latin it follows a verb of **saying, thinking, knowing or perceiving**.`},{kind:"table",caption:"Common verbs that introduce indirect statement",head:["Kind","Verbs"],rows:[["Saying","dīcō, nūntiō, respondeō, scrībō, negō, prōmittō, polliceor"],["Thinking","putō, crēdō, exīstimō, arbitror, spērō"],["Knowing","sciō, nesciō, intellegō"],["Perceiving","videō, audiō, sentiō"]]},{kind:"p",text:"Keep these apart from the verbs that introduce other reported speech. A verb of **commanding or asking** someone to do something (imperō, ōrō, hortor, rogō) takes an **indirect command**: ut or nē + subjunctive. A verb of **asking a question** (quaerō, rogō) takes an **indirect question**: a question word + subjunctive."}]},{heading:"Accusative Subject + Infinitive",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'Latin has **no word for "that"** in an indirect statement. Instead, the subject of the reported statement goes into the **accusative**, and its verb becomes an **infinitive**. Any adjective or participle describing that subject is accusative too.'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Putō **puerum aegrum esse**.",en:"I think that the boy is sick.",note:"Direct: Puer aeger est."},{la:"Sentiō **mē dēcipī**.",en:"I realise that I am being deceived."},{la:"Explōrātor nūntiāvit **Gallōs flūmen trānsīre**.",en:"The scout reported that the Gauls were crossing the river."}]},{kind:"p",text:'English usually adds "that", but it can be left out: "I think the boy is sick." Never translate the infinitive as "to" here.'}]},{heading:"The six infinitives",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:"A Latin verb has three tenses of infinitive, each active and passive. The perfect passive and the future active are built from participles, so they **agree with the accusative subject** in gender, number and case."},{kind:"table",caption:"Infinitives of laudō, laudāre, laudāvī, laudātum",head:["Tense","Active","Passive","Time"],rows:[["Present","laudāre, to praise","laudārī, to be praised","same time"],["Perfect","laudāvisse, to have praised","laudātus, -a, -um esse, to have been praised","before"],["Future","laudātūrus, -a, -um esse, to be about to praise","laudātum īrī (rare)","after"]]},{kind:"p",text:'The **present passive** infinitive ends in -ārī, -ērī and -īrī in the 1st, 2nd and 4th conjugations, but in plain **-ī** in the 3rd: dūcī, "to be led", capī, "to be taken". The **perfect active** is the perfect stem + **-isse**: cēpisse, vīdisse. In the participle forms, **esse** is often left out.'},{kind:"p",text:'A **deponent** has a present infinitive that is passive in form but active in meaning (hortārī, "to encourage"; loquī, "to speak"). Its perfect infinitive is its perfect participle + esse (locūtus esse, "to have spoken"), and its future is the usual future active (locūtūrus esse).'}]},{heading:"Present Infinitive: Same Time; Perfect: Time Before; Future: Time After",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:"The infinitive does not show time on its own. Like a participle, it shows time **relative to the main verb**. The English tense then depends on whether the main verb is present or past."},{kind:"table",head:["Infinitive","Time",'After dīcit ("he says")','After dīxit ("he said")'],rows:[["Present: advenīre","same time","that the consul is arriving","that the consul was arriving"],["Perfect: advēnisse","before","that the consul has arrived","that the consul had arrived"],["Future: adventūrum esse","after","that the consul will arrive","that the consul would arrive"]]},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Crēdēbant nāvēs ā pīrātīs **dēlētās esse**.",en:"They believed that the ships had been destroyed by the pirates.",note:"dēlētās agrees with nāvēs, feminine accusative plural."},{la:"Spērābant sē mox domum **reditūrōs esse**.",en:"They hoped that they would soon return home."},{la:"Pollicitus est sē pecūniam **redditūrum esse**.",en:"He promised that he would give back the money."}]},{kind:"p",text:'Verbs of **hoping, promising and threatening** look ahead, so they take the **future infinitive** with a subject accusative. English says "I hope to win" or "he promised to pay"; Latin says "I hope that I will win", "he promised that he would pay".'}]},{heading:"Reflexive sē and suus",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:`When the subject of the infinitive is the **same person** as the subject of the main verb, Latin uses the reflexive **sē**. When it is **someone else**, Latin uses a form of **is, ea, id** (eum, eam, eōs, eās). In the same way, the possessive **suus** means "his own, her own, their own", pointing back to the main subject, while **eius** and **eōrum** mean someone else's.`},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Rēgīna crēdēbat **sē** tūtam esse.",en:"The queen believed that she (herself) was safe."},{la:"Rēgīna crēdēbat **eam** tūtam esse.",en:"The queen believed that she (another woman) was safe."},{la:"Pater dīxit **suam** domum ārdēre.",en:"The father said that his own house was on fire."},{la:"Pater dīxit domum **eius** ārdēre.",en:"The father said that his (another man's) house was on fire."},{la:"Mīlitēs dīxērunt **sē** fessōs esse.",en:"The soldiers said that they were tired.",note:"sē is the same form in the singular and plural; fessōs shows it is masculine plural."}]}]},{heading:"negō: Say That ... Not",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'To report a negative statement, Latin normally uses **negō**, "say that ... not" or "deny", rather than dīcō with nōn. The "not" belongs in the English reported clause.'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Puer **negat** sē pecūniam cēpisse.",en:"The boy says that he did not take the money.",note:'Or: "The boy denies that he took the money."'},{la:"**Negāvērunt** hostēs pācem petītūrōs esse.",en:"They said that the enemy would not ask for peace."}]},{kind:"tip",title:'No "that" word, and watch the endings',text:'Do not write ut or quod for "that" after dīcō: the subject goes into the **accusative** and the verb into the **infinitive**. The participle in a compound infinitive must agree with that accusative subject: *Spērō amīcum mē adiūtūrum esse*, "I hope that my friend will help me", not adiūtūrus. And after a verb of hoping or promising, use the **future** infinitive, even where English says "to".'}]}],check:["m11-103","m11-303","m11-503","m11-601","m11-701"]},m={moduleId:"participles_ablative_absolute",title:"Participles and the Ablative Absolute",summary:"A participle is a verbal adjective: it agrees with a noun like an adjective, but it has tense and voice like a verb. Its tense shows time relative to the main verb. A noun and participle in the ablative, standing apart from the rest of the sentence, form an ablative absolute.",sections:[{heading:"Present Active, Perfect Passive and Future Active Participles",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'An ordinary Latin verb has three participles. The **present active** participle means "...ing". The **perfect passive** participle means "having been ...ed". The **future active** participle means "about to ..." or "going to ...". (The gerundive, sometimes called the future passive participle, has its own lesson in M12.)'},{kind:"table",caption:"Forming the participles",head:["Conjugation","Principal parts","Present active","Perfect passive","Future active"],rows:[["1st","laudō, laudāre, laudāvī, laudātum","laudāns, laudantis","laudātus, -a, -um","laudātūrus, -a, -um"],["2nd","habeō, habēre, habuī, habitum","habēns, habentis","habitus, -a, -um","habitūrus, -a, -um"],["3rd","dūcō, dūcere, dūxī, ductum","dūcēns, dūcentis","ductus, -a, -um","ductūrus, -a, -um"],["3rd -iō","faciō, facere, fēcī, factum","faciēns, facientis","factus, -a, -um","factūrus, -a, -um"],["4th","mūniō, mūnīre, mūnīvī, mūnītum","mūniēns, mūnientis","mūnītus, -a, -um","mūnītūrus, -a, -um"]]},{kind:"p",text:"The **present participle** adds **-ns** to the present stem: -āns in the 1st conjugation, -ēns in the 2nd and 3rd, -iēns in 3rd -iō and 4th. The vowel is long before -ns and short before -nt-, so the genitive is laudantis, dūcentis. It declines like a 3rd-declension adjective, with genitive plural **-ntium**. In the ablative singular it ends in **-e** when it acts as a participle (and always in an ablative absolute), but **-ī** when it is used as a plain adjective."},{kind:"p",text:'The **perfect passive** and **future active** participles both come from the **fourth principal part**. Drop the -um and add **-us** for the perfect passive or **-ūrus** for the future active; both decline like bonus, -a, -um. Use the fourth principal part even when it looks irregular: ferō, ferre, tulī, lātum gives lātus and lātūrus. The verb **sum** has no ordinary present participle and no perfect passive, but its future participle is **futūrus**, "about to be".'}]},{heading:"Participles of Deponent Verbs",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'A deponent verb is passive in form but active in meaning, and its participles follow the meaning. The **perfect participle** of a deponent is its last principal part without sum, and it is **active**: locūtus means "having spoken", not "having been spoken". Deponents also have an ordinary present participle (loquēns, "speaking") and future participle (locūtūrus, "about to speak").'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Lēgātus, multa **locūtus**, cōnsēdit.",en:"Having said many things, the envoy sat down.",note:"loquor, loquī, locūtus sum"},{la:"Mercātor, Rōmā **profectus**, Capuam pervēnit.",en:"Having set out from Rome, the merchant reached Capua.",note:"proficīscor, proficīscī, profectus sum"}]},{kind:"p",text:'This is worth noticing because ordinary verbs have **no perfect active participle**. Latin cannot say "having captured the city" with a single participle of capiō; it uses an ablative absolute or a cum clause instead. Only deponents can say "having ...ed" in one word.'}]},{heading:"Agreement and Relative Time",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:"A participle is an adjective, so it **agrees** with its noun in gender, number and case. Use the ending to find the noun it belongs to, even when the two are far apart."},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Pater fīliam **lacrimantem** vīdit.",en:"The father saw his daughter crying.",note:"lacrimantem is accusative singular, so it goes with fīliam, not pater."},{la:"Oppidānī **territī** portās clausērunt.",en:"The townspeople, terrified, shut the gates."}]},{kind:"p",text:"A participle has no time of its own. Its tense shows time **relative to the main verb**: the present participle shows the **same time**, the perfect shows time **before**, and the future shows time **after**."},{kind:"table",head:["Participle","Time compared with the main verb","Literal English","Often better as"],rows:[["Present active","same time","...ing","while / as he is (was) ...ing"],["Perfect passive","before","having been ...ed","after (when) he had been ...ed"],["Future active","after","about to ..., going to ...","as he was about to ..., intending to ..."]]},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Fēminae **flentēs** ex urbe exeunt.",en:"The women go out of the city weeping."},{la:"Fēminae **flentēs** ex urbe exiērunt.",en:"The women went out of the city weeping (while they were weeping).",note:'Same participle; the main verb is now past, so the English shifts to "were".'},{la:"Captīvī **līberātī** grātiās ēgērunt.",en:"Having been freed (after they had been freed), the prisoners gave thanks."},{la:"Nautae **nāvigātūrī** sacrificium fēcērunt.",en:"As they were about to sail, the sailors made a sacrifice."}]}]},{heading:"Ablative Absolute",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'An **ablative absolute** is a noun (or pronoun) and a participle, both in the **ablative**, with no preposition. "Absolute" means "loosened": the phrase is not the subject or object of the main verb and is not governed by any word in the sentence. It sets the scene for the main clause.'},{kind:"p",text:'Translate it literally first ("the gates having been shut"), then turn it into an English clause. The participle still shows relative time, and the context tells you the best linking word: **when, after, while, since, although** or **if**.'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"**Portīs clausīs**, oppidānī sē dēfendērunt.",en:"When the gates had been shut, the townspeople defended themselves."},{la:"**Patre dormiente**, puerī ē domō clam exiērunt.",en:"While their father was sleeping, the boys secretly went out of the house.",note:"Present participle in an ablative absolute: -e, not -ī."},{la:"**Sōle oriente**, nautae nāvem solvērunt.",en:"As the sun was rising, the sailors set sail."},{la:"**Multīs vulnerātīs**, Rōmānī tamen nōn cessērunt.",en:"Although many had been wounded, the Romans nevertheless did not give way.",note:'tamen points to "although".'},{la:"**Pecūniā āmissā**, mercātor domum rediit.",en:"Having lost his money, the merchant went home.",note:'English often turns the passive into an active: "the money having been lost" becomes "having lost his money".'}]}]},{heading:"Ablative Absolute without a Participle",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'Because **sum** has no present participle, Latin can make an ablative absolute from **two nouns**, or a **noun and an adjective**, with no participle at all. Supply "being" in a literal translation, then find a natural English phrase.'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"**Tarquiniō rēge**, Rōma nōndum lībera erat.",en:"When Tarquinius was king, Rome was not yet free.",note:'Literally "Tarquinius being king".'},{la:"**Tē duce**, vincēmus.",en:"With you as leader, we will win."},{la:"**Patre invītō**, fīlius mīles factus est.",en:"Against his father's will, the son became a soldier."}]}]},{heading:"Is it really absolute?",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:"Latin does **not** use an ablative absolute when its noun is also the subject or object of the main verb. The participle simply agrees with that noun. And a noun and participle in the ablative after a **preposition** (in, ex, cum, ā) are an ordinary prepositional phrase, not an ablative absolute."},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Rōmānī oppidum **captum** incendērunt.",en:"The Romans set fire to the town they had captured.",note:"oppidum is the object of incendērunt, so no ablative absolute."},{la:"Cīvēs **ex oppidō captō** fūgērunt.",en:"The citizens fled from the captured town.",note:"Governed by ex: not absolute."}]},{kind:"tip",title:"Three traps",text:'In an ablative absolute the present participle ends in **-e** (*patre dormiente*), not -ī. A **deponent** perfect participle is **active**: *secūtus* is "having followed", not "having been followed". And a present participle with a **past** main verb is translated "while they **were** ...ing", not "are".'}]}],check:["m10-203","m10-303","m10-401","m10-501","m10-502"]},p={moduleId:"purpose_result_indirect",title:"Purpose, Result and Indirect Command",summary:"Three kinds of clause begin with ut and use the subjunctive. Learn to tell them apart from the words in the main clause and from the negative each one takes.",sections:[{heading:"Three ut clauses",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:"A purpose clause, a result clause and an indirect command can look identical: **ut + subjunctive**. You cannot tell them apart from the ut clause alone. Look back at the **main clause** for a clue, and look at the **negative**."},{kind:"p",text:'All three follow the sequence of tenses with the "same time" column: **present subjunctive** after a primary main verb, **imperfect subjunctive** after a secondary one. (A result clause after a past main verb sometimes uses the perfect subjunctive to stress that the result really happened, but present and imperfect are the usual pattern.)'}]},{heading:"Purpose Clauses",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'A purpose clause says **why** someone does something: the aim of the action. It uses **ut** for a positive purpose and **nē** for a negative one. English often uses "to" or "in order to", or "so that... may / might". For nē, say "so that... not" or "lest".'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Pater fīliō pecūniam dat **ut librōs emat**.",en:"The father gives his son money so that he may buy books."},{la:"Ad forum festīnāvimus **ut amīcōs vidērēmus**.",en:"We hurried to the forum to see our friends."},{la:"Portās claudunt **nē hostēs intrent**.",en:"They are shutting the gates so that the enemy may not enter."}]},{kind:"p",text:'There is no special signal word in the main clause. If nothing points to a result or a command and the clause answers "why?", it is purpose. Classical Latin prose does not use a plain infinitive to show purpose; the usual way is ut + subjunctive.'},{kind:"p",text:"When the main clause has a noun the purpose clause can refer back to, Latin often uses a relative pronoun instead of ut. This is a **relative clause of purpose**, and you will study it fully in M9."},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Caesar equitēs praemīsit **quī locum explōrārent**.",en:"Caesar sent cavalry ahead to scout the place.",note:"quī refers back to equitēs."}]}]},{heading:"Result Clauses",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'A result clause says what **happened because of** something: the consequence. The main clause almost always contains a signal word meaning "so" or "such".'},{kind:"table",caption:"Result signal words in the main clause",head:["Signal word","Meaning"],rows:[["tam","so (with adjectives and adverbs)"],["tantus, -a, -um","so great, such a great"],["tālis, -e","such, of such a kind"],["ita","so, in such a way"],["sīc","so, thus"],["adeō","to such an extent, so much"]]},{kind:"p",text:'The positive is **ut**. The negative keeps the ut and adds a negative word: **ut nōn**, **ut nēmō** ("that no one"), **ut nihil** ("that nothing"), **ut numquam** ("that never"). Translate the verb as a plain English verb ("beat", "hear"), not with "may" or "might", because the result is a fact.'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Puella **tam** celeriter cucurrit **ut omnēs superāret**.",en:"The girl ran so fast that she beat everyone."},{la:"**Tantus** clāmor est **ut nihil audiāmus**.",en:"The noise is so great that we hear nothing."},{la:"Mīlitēs **adeō** territī erant **ut nōn pugnārent**.",en:"The soldiers were so frightened that they did not fight."},{la:"**Tālis** vir est **ut omnēs eum ament**.",en:"He is such a man that everyone loves him."}]},{kind:"p",text:'Some verbs take a whole result clause as their subject or object: **accidit ut** ("it happens that"), **fit ut** ("it comes about that"), **efficiō ut** ("I bring it about that"). This is a **substantive clause of result**. It still uses ut and ut nōn.'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Accidit **ut cōnsul aegrōtāret**.",en:"It happened that the consul was ill."}]}]},{heading:"Indirect Commands",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'An indirect command reports an order, request, warning or piece of advice. The main verb is a verb of **ordering, asking, begging, urging, warning or persuading**. It uses **ut** for a positive command and **nē** for a negative one. English usually translates with "to" or "not to": "I begged them not to leave."'},{kind:"table",caption:"Common verbs that take an indirect command",head:["Verb","Meaning","Person told or asked"],rows:[["imperō, imperāre","order, command","dative"],["persuādeō, persuādēre","persuade","dative"],["moneō, monēre","warn, advise","accusative"],["hortor, hortārī","urge, encourage","accusative"],["ōrō, ōrāre","beg","accusative"],["rogō, rogāre","ask","accusative"],["petō, petere","ask, request","ā / ab + ablative"]]},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Dux mīlitibus **imperat ut castra mūniant**.",en:"The general orders the soldiers to fortify the camp."},{la:"Amīcum **hortātus sum ut fortis esset**.",en:"I urged my friend to be brave."},{la:"Nautās **ōrāvimus nē nāvem relinquerent**.",en:"We begged the sailors not to abandon the ship."}]},{kind:"tip",title:"iubeō takes an infinitive",text:'**iubeō** ("order") does not take ut. It takes an accusative and an infinitive, just like English: *Dux mīlitēs iussit castra mūnīre*, "The general ordered the soldiers to fortify the camp." Its opposite **vetō** ("forbid") works the same way.'}]},{heading:"Telling them apart",blocks:[{kind:"table",head:["","Purpose","Result","Indirect command"],rows:[["Clue in main clause",'none; answers "why?"',"tam, tantus, tālis, ita, sīc, adeō","verb of ordering, asking, urging"],["Positive","ut","ut","ut"],["Negative","nē","ut nōn, ut nēmō, ut nihil","nē"],["English","to..., so that... may","so... that (plain verb)","to..., not to..."]]},{kind:"tip",title:"Check the main clause, then the negative",text:'First scan the main clause: a "so" word means **result**, a verb of ordering or asking means **indirect command**, and nothing at all means **purpose**. Then check the negative. Result is the only one that keeps ut and says **ut nōn**; purpose and indirect command both use **nē**. In a purpose clause, nē means "so that... not" or "lest", never just "that".'}]}],check:["m3-204","m3-302","m3-401","m3-604","m3-706"]},v={moduleId:"quin_clauses",title:"Quīn Clauses",summary:"Quīn introduces a subjunctive clause after a negative expression of doubting or preventing. Learn what it means after each kind of verb, and what Latin uses instead when the main verb is not negative.",sections:[{heading:"Quīn needs a negative",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'Quīn comes from an old word for "how" joined to a negative, so it carries a "not" inside it. That is why a quīn clause depends on a main clause that is itself **negative**: nōn, nēmō, nihil, or a question that expects the answer "no", such as quis dubitat? ("who doubts?", meaning "no one doubts").'},{kind:"p",text:"The verb in a quīn clause is **subjunctive** and follows the sequence of tenses: present or perfect after a primary main verb, imperfect or pluperfect after a secondary one."}]},{heading:"Doubting: nōn dubitō quīn",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'After a **negative expression of doubting** (nōn dubitō, nēmō dubitat, quis dubitat?), quīn means simply **"that"**. English does not translate its built-in negative: "I do not doubt **that** ...".'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Nōn dubitō **quīn** frāter tuus **vēnerit**.",en:"I do not doubt that your brother has come.",note:"Primary, time before: perfect subjunctive."},{la:"Nēmō dubitābat **quīn** nūntius vērum **dīceret**.",en:"No one doubted that the messenger was telling the truth.",note:"Secondary, same time: imperfect subjunctive."},{la:"Quis dubitat **quīn** hic labor difficilis **sit**?",en:"Who doubts that this task is difficult?",note:'A question expecting "no one" counts as a negative.'}]},{kind:"p",text:'A **positive** dubitō ("I doubt") does not take quīn. It takes an indirect question, usually with **num** ("whether"). And nōn dubitō with an **infinitive** means "I do not hesitate to ...".'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Dubitō **num** haec fābula vēra **sit**.",en:"I doubt whether this story is true."},{la:"Nōn dubitāvit flūmen **trānsīre**.",en:"He did not hesitate to cross the river."}]}]},{heading:"Preventing: quīn, quōminus and nē",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'Verbs of **preventing, hindering and deterring** (impediō, dēterreō, retineō, obstō) take a subjunctive clause that English translates **"from ...ing"**. Which word introduces it depends on whether the main verb is negative.'},{kind:"table",head:["Main verb","Clause begins with","Example"],rows:[["positive: impedit, dēterret","nē or quōminus","He prevents me from going."],["negative: nōn impedit, nihil impedit, nēmō dēterret","quīn or quōminus","Nothing prevents me from going."]]},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Tempestās nōs impedīvit **nē nāvigārēmus**.",en:"The storm prevented us from sailing."},{la:"Morbus eum impedit **quōminus labōret**.",en:"Illness prevents him from working."},{la:"Nēmō mē dēterrēbit **quīn** vērum **dīcam**.",en:"No one will deter me from telling the truth.",note:"Negative main clause, so quīn."},{la:"Nihil obstābat **quīn** mīlitēs flūmen **trānsīrent**.",en:"Nothing stood in the way of the soldiers crossing the river."}]},{kind:"p",text:'Translate the subjunctive with an English "-ing" form after "from": quīn dīcam, "from telling". Do not translate it as "that".'}]},{heading:"Other negative expressions",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'A few fixed phrases also take quīn. Here quīn keeps its "not", so English needs "that ... not" or a positive rewording.'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"**Fierī nōn potest quīn** dē hāc rē **audiās**.",en:"It cannot be that you do not hear about this. (You are bound to hear about this.)"},{la:"Nēmō est **quīn** hoc **sciat**.",en:"There is no one who does not know this.",note:"Here quīn stands for quī nōn."}]}]},{heading:"Recognising a quīn clause",blocks:[{kind:"table",head:["Main clause","Construction","English"],rows:[["nōn dubitō, nēmō dubitat, quis dubitat?","quīn + subjunctive","that ..."],["dubitō (positive)","num + subjunctive","whether ..."],["negative verb of preventing","quīn or quōminus + subjunctive","from ...ing"],["positive verb of preventing","nē or quōminus + subjunctive","from ...ing"],["fierī nōn potest, nēmō est","quīn + subjunctive","that ... not, who ... not"]]},{kind:"tip",title:"Look for the negative first",text:'Quīn appears only after a **negative** or a question expecting "no". If the main clause says *nōn dubitō* or *nēmō dubitat*, quīn means "that". If it says plain *dubitō*, expect *num*, "whether", and an indirect question instead. If it is a verb of preventing, translate "from ...ing", and expect quīn only when that verb is negated.'}]}],check:["m8-201","m8-203","m8-302","m8-303"]},b={moduleId:"relative_subjunctive",title:"Relative Clauses with the Subjunctive",summary:"Most relative clauses state a fact and use the indicative. With the subjunctive, a relative clause can express a purpose or describe the kind of person or thing meant. Learn the signals for each.",sections:[{heading:"Fact or something more?",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:"An ordinary relative clause (quī, quae, quod) gives a **fact** about its antecedent, and its verb is **indicative**. When the verb of a relative clause is **subjunctive**, the clause is doing something more: it tells you **why** something was done (purpose) or **what sort** of person or thing is meant (characteristic)."},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Mīles quī prope portam **stābat** nihil vīdit.",en:"The soldier who was standing near the gate saw nothing.",note:"Indicative: a plain fact about one soldier."}]},{kind:"p",text:"Relative subjunctives follow the sequence of tenses like other dependent clauses."}]},{heading:"Relative Purpose",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'In the M3 lesson you met the relative clause of purpose. When the main clause has a noun that the purpose clause can refer back to, Latin often uses a relative pronoun instead of ut: **quī = ut is** ("so that he"). The antecedent is usually the object of a verb of **sending, choosing or giving**. English normally translates with "to".'},{kind:"p",text:"Like any purpose clause, it looks forward from the main verb, so it uses only the **present** subjunctive (primary sequence) or the **imperfect** (secondary sequence)."},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Rēx servum mīsit **quī** medicum **arcesseret**.",en:"The king sent a slave to fetch the doctor."},{la:"Cōnsul virōs dēligit **quī** mūrōs **custōdiant**.",en:"The consul is choosing men to guard the walls."},{la:"Librum tibi dabō **quem legās**.",en:"I will give you a book to read.",note:"The relative pronoun takes its own case: quem is the object of legās."},{la:"Locum quaerēbant **ubi** castra **pōnerent**.",en:"They were looking for a place to pitch camp.",note:"A relative adverb such as ubi can do the same job."}]},{kind:"p",text:'With a **comparative** adjective or adverb, a purpose clause is introduced by **quō** ("so that by this much") instead of ut.'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Mūrum altiōrem fēcērunt **quō** facilius urbem **dēfenderent**.",en:"They made the wall higher so that they could defend the city more easily."}]}]},{heading:"Relative Characteristic",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'A relative clause of **characteristic** describes the **kind** of person or thing meant, not a particular one. The antecedent is usually general, indefinite or negative, and it comes in a few set patterns. English often translates with a plain verb; when the idea of "the sort who would" matters, bring it out.'},{kind:"table",head:["Signal","Meaning"],rows:[["sunt quī, est quī","there are people who, there is someone who"],["nēmō est quī, nihil est quod","there is no one who, there is nothing that"],["quis est quī ...?","who is there who ...?"],["sōlus est quī, ūnus est quī","he is the only one who"],["nōn is sum quī","I am not the sort of man who"]]},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"**Sunt quī** nihil **timeant**.",en:"There are people who fear nothing."},{la:"**Quis est quī** hunc virum nōn **laudet**?",en:"Who is there who would not praise this man?"},{la:"**Nēmō** aderat **quī** Graecē **loquerētur**.",en:"There was no one present who spoke Greek."},{la:"Tū **sōla es quae** mē adiuvāre **possīs**.",en:"You are the only one who can help me."},{la:"**Nōn is sum quī** perīcula **fugiam**.",en:"I am not the sort of man who runs away from danger."}]},{kind:"p",text:'A characteristic clause is not limited to the "same time" column. It can use the perfect or pluperfect for an action before the main verb.'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Nūllus erat mīles **quī** Rōmam **vīdisset**.",en:"There was not a single soldier who had seen Rome.",note:"Secondary sequence, time before: pluperfect subjunctive."}]}]},{heading:"Worthy and fit: dignus quī",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'The adjectives **dignus** ("worthy"), **indignus** ("unworthy") and **idōneus** ("suitable, fit") are followed by **quī + subjunctive**. English uses an infinitive: "worthy **to** ...", "fit **to** ...".'},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Haec fābula **digna est quae nārrētur**.",en:"This story deserves to be told."},{la:"Puer **idōneus** nōn erat **quī** nāvem **regeret**.",en:"The boy was not fit to steer the ship."}]}]},{heading:"Telling them apart",blocks:[{kind:"table",head:["Clause","Antecedent or signal","Mood","English"],rows:[["Simple relative","a particular person or thing","indicative","who, which (a fact)"],["Relative purpose","object of sending, choosing, giving","present or imperfect subjunctive","to ..., so that he might ..."],["Characteristic","sunt quī, nēmō est quī, sōlus est quī","subjunctive","who (the sort who would)"],["dignus quī","dignus, indignus, idōneus","subjunctive","worthy to, fit to"]]},{kind:"tip",title:"Check the mood first",text:'A relative clause with an **indicative** verb is a plain fact: *Hic est mīles quī urbem servāvit*, "This is the soldier who saved the city." Only a **subjunctive** makes it purpose or characteristic. Then look at the main clause: a verb of sending or choosing points to **purpose**; an indefinite or negative antecedent (sunt quī, nēmō est quī) or sōlus points to **characteristic**.'}]}],check:["m9-201","m9-202","m9-204","m9-304"]},f={moduleId:"sequence_of_tenses",title:"Sequence of Tenses",summary:"The tense of a subjunctive in a dependent clause is not a free choice. It depends on two things: the tense of the main verb, and whether the subjunctive action happens at the same time as the main verb or before it.",sections:[{heading:"The big idea",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:"Most subjunctives sit in a dependent clause attached to a main verb. To pick (or identify) the right subjunctive tense, ask two questions in order:"},{kind:"p",text:"**1. What tense is the main verb?** This puts the sentence in **primary** or **secondary** sequence. **2. When does the subjunctive action happen compared with the main verb?** At the **same time**, or **before** it? This is called **relative time**."},{kind:"p",text:"Two answers to the first question and two to the second give four boxes, and each box has exactly one subjunctive tense in it."}]},{heading:"Primary Sequence",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:"A main verb in the **present, future or future perfect** tense (or a command) looks at the present or the future. That puts the sentence in **primary sequence**, which uses the **present** and **perfect** subjunctive."},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Quaerō cūr puer **lacrimet**.",en:"I ask why the boy is crying.",note:"Present main verb, same time: present subjunctive."},{la:"Quaerō cūr puer **lacrimāverit**.",en:"I ask why the boy cried.",note:"Present main verb, time before: perfect subjunctive."},{la:"Mox intellegēs quid **agāmus**.",en:"Soon you will understand what we are doing.",note:"Future main verb, so this is primary too."}]}]},{heading:"Secondary Sequence",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:"A main verb in the **imperfect, perfect or pluperfect** tense looks at the past. That puts the sentence in **secondary sequence** (also called historical sequence), which uses the **imperfect** and **pluperfect** subjunctive."},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Quaerēbam cūr puer **lacrimāret**.",en:"I was asking why the boy was crying.",note:"Imperfect main verb, same time: imperfect subjunctive."},{la:"Quaesīvī cūr puer **lacrimāvisset**.",en:"I asked why the boy had cried.",note:"Perfect main verb, time before: pluperfect subjunctive."},{la:"Cognōverant quis portam **aperuisset**.",en:"They had found out who had opened the gate.",note:"Pluperfect main verb, so this is secondary too."}]}]},{heading:"Relative Time: same time or time before",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:'Relative time means time compared with the main verb, not compared with today. "Same time" means the subjunctive action is going on while the main action happens. "Time before" means it was already finished by then.'},{kind:"table",caption:"The sequence of tenses",head:["Main verb","Sequence","Same time","Time before"],rows:[["present, future, future perfect, command","Primary","present subjunctive","perfect subjunctive"],["imperfect, perfect, pluperfect","Secondary","imperfect subjunctive","pluperfect subjunctive"]]},{kind:"table",caption:"The same four boxes with real forms (amō, 3rd sg.)",head:["Sequence","Same time","Time before"],rows:[["Primary","amet / amētur","amāverit / amātus sit"],["Secondary","amāret / amārētur","amāvisset / amātus esset"]]},{kind:"p",text:'In English, same time comes out as "is doing" (primary) or "was doing" (secondary). Time before comes out as "did" or "has done" (primary) or "had done" (secondary).'},{kind:"p",text:"Deponent verbs follow the same table with their passive-looking forms: loquātur, locūtus sit (primary); loquerētur, locūtus esset (secondary)."}]},{heading:"Putting it to work",blocks:[{kind:"p",text:"Purpose clauses and indirect commands always point forward from the main verb, so they only ever use the **same time** column: present subjunctive in primary sequence, imperfect in secondary."},{kind:"examples",items:[{la:"Clāmāmus ut nōs **audiant**.",en:"We are shouting so that they may hear us.",note:"Present main verb: present subjunctive."},{la:"Clāmāvimus ut nōs **audīrent**.",en:"We shouted so that they might hear us.",note:"Perfect main verb: imperfect subjunctive."}]},{kind:"tip",title:"Read a two-word passive as one verb",text:'A participle plus a form of esse is a single verb, and the esse form tells you its tense. **missus sit** is perfect subjunctive (primary, time before). **missus esset** is pluperfect subjunctive (secondary, time before). Do not see esset and answer "imperfect, same time".'},{kind:"tip",title:"The historical present",text:"Writers often tell a past story in the present tense to make it vivid. A historical present can take either sequence. **Secondary** sequence is very common, because the events are really past, but primary sequence also appears. To see which one the writer chose, look at the tense of the subjunctive."}]}],check:["m1-303","m1-402","m1-602","m1-603","m1-605"]},g={forms_bootcamp:u,sequence_of_tenses:f,independent_uses:d,purpose_result_indirect:p,cum_clauses:o,indirect_question:l,fear_clauses:r,conditionals:s,quin_clauses:v,relative_subjunctive:b,participles_ablative_absolute:m,indirect_statement:c,gerund_gerundive:h,case_uses:a};function w(e){return g[e]}const n="latiniq_lessons_read";function i(){try{const e=JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(n)??"[]");return Array.isArray(e)?e.filter(t=>typeof t=="string"):[]}catch{return[]}}function k(e){return i().includes(e)}function y(e){try{const t=i();if(t.includes(e))return;localStorage.setItem(n,JSON.stringify([...t,e]))}catch{}}export{k as h,w as l,y as m};
